{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"STOPPED — needs a design decision. The prescribed destination (`adapters/jira/`) is blocked by a\nchecked-in architectural invariant, and every obvious alternative destination is blocked by a\ndifferent one. No option is mechanically correct; picking one is a judgment call, so I am leaving\nthis open rather than inventing an answer.\n\n## What I did\n\nPerformed the full move on a scratch branch off `origin/main` (c89a2ddcc4): `git mv` to\n`adapters/jira/inbound_fields.py`, repointed `_load_adf`'s sibling path, repointed all 11\nproduction/test/script importers (established with BOTH Serena `find_referencing_symbols` AND a\nraw grep for the module name as a STRING — which is what caught\n`importlib.import_module(\"rebar_reconciler.inbound_fields\")` in\n`test_inbound_parent_clear_88d9.py:438`, the `_INBOUND_FIELDS_PATH` by-path load in\n`test_inbound_unmapped_status_no_reopen.py:40`, and the `_recon(\"inbound_fields\")` /\n`_JIRA_ADAPTER_MODULES` by-name load in `test_reconcile_live_e2e.py`), removed\n`_SWEEP_EXEMPTIONS` and `test_sweep_exemption_is_still_needed`, and fixed the `_ADF_ALLOWLIST`\nkey in `test_rich_text_seam_heldout.py`.\n\nResult: 124 passed, 1 failed. The neutrality sweep gate DOES pass with zero exemptions. The\nfailure is elsewhere, and it is not a mechanical oversight.\n\n## The blocker (measured, not inferred)\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py::test_import_graph_contract_holds_for_every_engine_module`\n\n```\nAssertionError: import contract violated:\n  rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira_datacenter imports sibling adapter package\n  rebar_reconciler.adapters.jira.\n```\n\nStory J7 seals the two concrete adapter packages against each other **in both directions**\n(`_violations`, the `for near, far in ((_JIRA, _DC), (_DC, _JIRA))` loop) — the stated intent is\nthat \"a concrete backend reaching sideways for the other's vendor internals\" must instead share\nthrough `adapters/jira_family/`.\n\nBut `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:123-134` genuinely shares this module — its own\ndocstring says \"Delegates to the SAME `inbound_fields` mapper Cloud uses\". So the module's\nresidency at the package root is not merely cosmetic: **it is what currently keeps the DC->Cloud\nseal intact.** The sweep exemption 556a exists to delete is the visible price of that.\n\nThe obvious alternative destination fails the mirror-image rule. I ran the real `_violations`\npredicate against a hypothetical `adapters/jira_family/inbound_fields.py`:\n\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter` importing it -> OK\n- `rebar_reconciler` (root) importing it -> **violation**: \"only adapters/jira/ and\n  adapters/jira_datacenter/ may consume the Jira-family layer\"\n\nand `inbound_differ.py:36` (the re-export block) and `peer_state.py:34`\n(`normalize_baseline_value`) are exactly such root-core importers.\n\nSo: `adapters/jira/` breaks the sibling seal; `adapters/jira_family/` breaks the\nfamily-consumer rule; the package root is where it is today and needs the sweep exemption. No\ndestination is contract-clean without additional design work.\n\n## The decision needed\n\nWhich of these is the intended resolution?\n\n**A. `adapters/jira_family/` + route the two root-core importers off it.**\n`inbound_differ.py`'s import is an explicitly-labelled re-export-for-attribute-access block, and\n`peer_state.py` needs one function (`normalize_baseline_value`). Both could plausibly move\nbehind the injected `InboundMapper` port that ticket 4af8 started. Cost: touches core wiring, so\nno longer a pure relocation. Also unresolved: the module lazy-loads `adapters/jira/adf.py` **by\npath** (a string the AST contract cannot see), so it would sit in the shared layer while still\nreaching for a Cloud-pinned vendor module — `_FORBIDDEN_IN_FAMILY` bans exactly that by name for\nimports, and the rule says such a dependency \"must arrive as an injected contract\". Passing the\ngate via a by-path load would be laundering, not compliance.\n\n**B. `adapters/jira/` + give DC its own inbound mapper.** Honours the seal literally. But the DC\nbackend deliberately shares Cloud's mapper today; splitting it is a behaviour-relevant change to\nDC inbound mapping, not a move.\n\n**C. `adapters/jira/` + amend the J7 contract to allow DC -> jira for this module.** Smallest\ndiff, but it deliberately re-opens a seam J7 closed on purpose (\"asymmetry would leave the seam\nhalf-open\"), and needs whoever owns that ADR to agree.\n\n**D. Split the module.** ADF/Cloud-specific parts stay in `adapters/jira/`; the genuinely shared\nparts (`_map_jira_to_local_fields`, `_identity_of`, `normalize_baseline_value`, the value maps)\ngo to `adapters/jira_family/`. Most faithful to the layering, largest change, and still needs\nA's answer for the root-core importers.\n\n**E. Close as won't-do** and instead re-justify the sweep exemption as the deliberate price of\nthe DC/Cloud seal, since the exemption is arguably recording a real constraint rather than debt.\n\nI have made no such choice. Nothing was pushed; the scratch branch is local only and the ticket\nis returned to `open`."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786097308075544001,"uuid":"81a5f3c9-724a-4f80-b9b2-97e1d34616e5"}